from mk import mk
mk("Pd9AFeTcl2k","A new Synth for Physical Modeling: reFX Rippler",
 "modal-partial-resonator-exciter-material-tone-hit-inharmonic-series-parallel-velocity-random-hit",
 "Modal (partial-bank) physical-modelling voice: exciter into one or two resonators defined by a partial model, with material (decay vs frequency), tone (level tilt), hit (random partial emphasis), inharmonic stretch, parallel or series resonators, and velocity/random modulation for a playable mallet or string",
 "Build a playable modal physical-modelling instrument from an exciter and a resonator made of tuned partials. Pick a partial model (string, beam, membrane, vibraphone, open tube or a manual partial list with 1 to 64 partials) and shape it with four relationships from the transcript: decay/release sets how long partials ring; material sets how fast high partials decay relative to low ones (muted vs glassy); tone sets partial level only (a static tilt, different from material); hit changes which partials are boosted at random, like plucking at a different position; and an inharmonic multiplier stretches or compresses partial spacing away from integer harmonics (metallic vs compressed). Choose the exciter (impulse, long exciter, noise): it mostly sets the attack, and a bass-heavy exciter mostly excites low partials. Run two resonators in parallel (blend) or in series (resonator 1 as an extended exciter, e.g. a short-decay drum head into a tube, with optional cross-feedback). Make it playable: velocity to level, material and exciter level; an envelope for a sharper attack over the ringing tail; subtle random on tone, stronger random on hit per note; key → layer gain to lift quiet high notes. More specific than our Karplus-Strong and comb-resonator skills: a modal partial bank with decay-vs-frequency and inharmonicity controls.",
 ["Modal/physical-modelling synth with exciter + resonator sections (several free and paid options exist), or Web Audio: a bank of tuned BiquadFilter bandpass (high Q) or decaying sine partials excited by a short noise/impulse buffer (implementation note)","Modulation sources: velocity, envelopes, per-note random, key tracking","Compressor, chorus and reverb for finishing"],
 [("partial_model","enum","string | beam | membrane | vibraphone | open tube | manual partial list",True),("partial_count","integer","1 to 64 partials (more = richer, more CPU)",False),("exciter","enum","impulse | long exciter | noise",True),("routing","enum","parallel (R1+R2) | series (R1→R2)",False)],
 [("modal_voice","patch","Playable modal mallet/string/tube instrument"),("mod_map","table","Velocity, envelope, random and key-tracking assignments")],
 ["Relationships come from the transcript: material = decay timing per partial, tone = partial level only, hit = random partial emphasis, inharmonic multiplier = partial spacing. No numeric presets are given in the video, so set values by ear.","Series mode with high cross-feedback strength can blow up or kill the sound. Raise it slowly.","Sponsored walkthrough. Price and opinion content dropped; the procedure is generic to modal synths."],
 [("Mute the resonators and raise the exciter's direct output to audition exciters (impulse, long exciter, noise). Then turn the direct output back down and enable resonator 1.","exciter","You hear the exciter alone, then only the resonated sound"),
  ("Pick a partial model and partial count for resonator 1 (start with the string model). Try beam, membrane, vibraphone and open tube to hear how partial patterns change the instrument.","resonator model","Clearly different instrument families from the same exciter"),
  ("Shape the partials: decay/release for ring time; material down = high partials die faster (muted), up = the opposite; tone = cut highs or lows by level only; hit = random partial emphasis (pluck position); inharmonic multiplier up = stretched, metallic partials, down = compressed partials.","material/tone/hit/inharm","Each control changes timbre in a distinct way; material changes decay over time, tone changes static brightness"),
  ("Optional manual mode: edit partial positions and amplitudes (e.g. 4 partials), make two snapshots and use a ratio control to morph between them.","manual partials","Custom partial set that can morph"),
  ("Add resonator 2. Parallel: same exciter into both, set level and balance. Series: exciter → R1 → R2; use R1 with a very short decay (drum head) as an extended exciter for a tube in R2. Raise cross-feedback strength carefully.","dual resonator","Parallel gives a blend; series gives a new hybrid body"),
  ("Optionally layer the two basic oscillators and route chosen sources through two filters (series or parallel), e.g. filter only the oscillators. Use the second layer for a second, different sound.","layering","Richer hybrid without muddying the resonators"),
  ("Make it playable: velocity → resonator level, material (louder = more high partials) and exciter level; envelope 1 with a fast attack and low sustain/decay → level for a prominent attack over the tail; subtle random → tone; per-note random → hit; for a vibraphone, slow sine LFO → pitch at a very small depth.","modulation","Dynamic, slightly varied notes like a real instrument"),
  ("Finish: low-cut a resonator if it gets muddy, then stereo widener, compressor, chorus and reverb. If high notes are quieter, map key tracking → layer gain.","fx + balance","Even level across the keyboard and a finished space")],
 ["With the resonators off and direct output up, only the exciter is heard; with direct output down, only the resonated tone is heard.","Turning material down makes high partials die faster than low ones, while tone changes brightness without changing decay length.","Raising the inharmonic multiplier gives an audibly metallic, bell-like tone.","Soft and hard notes differ in brightness and level; repeated notes vary slightly (random hit)."],
 ["physical-modelling","modal-synthesis","resonator","exciter","mallet","inharmonicity","velocity","web-audio"],
 note="Sponsored walkthrough; sponsor, price and opinion parts dropped.")

mk("B3n9GOT0Q9A","Syngular: Pianoteq's New Physically Modeled Synth // Walkthrough & Demo",
 "struck-string-model-pre-strike-brightness-frequency-dependent-decay-slope-mallet-bounce-polyrhythm-layers",
 "Struck-string physical-model synth controls that differ from subtractive synthesis: brightness set before the strike (not a filter), frequency-dependent decay (cutoff + slope) instead of sustain, strike point, harmonicity, three-string unison, and mallet-bounce retriggers at different note values per layer for sequenced polyrhythmic patches",
 "Design pads, basses and keys on a struck/plucked physical model and know which controls behave unlike a subtractive synth. Plucking brightness changes a sine-like tone into a saw-like one but only at the strike: lowering it while a note rings does nothing, so put a real filter after the model for sweeps. There is no sustain: decay and release plus a decay cutoff and slope define how partials above the cutoff die faster (low cutoff + high slope = more decay). Strike point acts like the pick position; harmonicity moves the tone from harmonic to inharmonic (steel-drum-like with more bloom); unison width detunes the three strings per note; smear softens transients. Mallet bounce makes a held note re-strike itself; stacking layers with different bounce values (dotted half, quarter, eight beats in the demo) gives sequenced, polyrhythmic patterns without a sequencer. Delta beyond our modal-partial skill: decay slope, pre-strike brightness and mallet-bounce layering.",
 ["Physical-model synth with struck-string/mallet engine, or Web Audio: Karplus/modal voice whose excitation lowpass is set per note, plus per-partial decay scaled above a cutoff (implementation note)","Post filter and EQ for sweeps","Layering (2–3 layers) for bounce polyrhythms"],
 [("brightness","number","Pre-strike brightness (sine-like → saw-like)",True),("decay_cutoff","number","Frequency above which partials decay faster",True),("decay_slope","number","How much faster partials above the cutoff decay",True),("bounce_values","list","Mallet-bounce note values per layer, e.g. dotted half, quarter, 8 beats",False)],
 [("struck_model_patch","patch","Physical-model pad/bass/keys patch"),("bounce_layers","patch","2–3 layer self-sequencing polyrhythmic patch")],
 ["Pre-strike brightness cannot be modulated like a filter cutoff after the note starts. Use a post filter for sweeps.","No sustain stage: shape length with decay, release, the decay cutoff/slope and the sustain pedal.","Walkthrough of a licensed product; the editable parameters need a higher licence tier in that product. Affiliate and licence talk dropped. Preset names not copied.","Values were spoken as shapes (low/high), apart from the bounce note values. Set the rest by ear."],
 [("Start from a sine-like init voice. Raise plucking/strike brightness to move toward a bright, saw-like tone. Test that turning it down while a note rings does not change that note.","brightness","Brightness changes only on the next strike"),
  ("Edit the harmonic profile (raise some partials, lower others) for a new basic colour, and set unison width to detune the three strings per note for synth-style thickness.","profile + unison","Thicker, chorus-like tone without a chorus effect"),
  ("Shape length: fast attack for keys or slower for pads; decay and release for ring time. Set the decay cutoff and slope: partials above the cutoff die faster as slope rises (low cutoff + high slope = shortest, darkest tail).","decay slope","Short presses show faster high-frequency decay with a higher slope"),
  ("Set strike point (like pick position) and harmonicity (harmonic → inharmonic). With higher brightness and more bloom, inharmonic settings give steel-drum-like tones. Use smear to soften transients.","strike + harmonicity","From pure tones to metallic drums"),
  ("Enable mallet bounce so a held note re-strikes; adjust bounce rate and decay over time.","bounce","A held note bounces rhythmically"),
  ("Build a sequenced patch with 2–3 layers and different bounce note values (e.g. dotted half, quarter, 8 beats). Turning off bounce on one layer will not stop the pattern while the other layers still bounce.","polyrhythm layers","Self-sequencing interlocking pattern from one held chord"),
  ("Map mod wheel → LFO rate, vibrato and tremolo depth. Finish with EQ and effects (chorus, phaser, delay, compression) and a post filter if you need cutoff sweeps.","performance + fx","Playable, finished patch")],
 ["Changing brightness during a held note has no audible effect; the next note changes.","With a higher decay slope, high partials fade noticeably faster on a short press.","A held chord on the 3-layer bounce patch produces an interlocking rhythm.","Raising harmonicity away from harmonic produces an audibly inharmonic, metallic tone."],
 ["physical-modelling","struck-string","mallet-bounce","polyrhythm","decay-slope","inharmonicity","layering","web-audio"],
 note="Product walkthrough with affiliate mention; licence and preset-tour content dropped.")

mk("xil_HpL1izg","Why This Classic Filter Sweep Still Sounds So Good",
 "classic-fin-envelope-filter-sweep-dual-detuned-osc-mono-8-voice-unison-tape-echo-dual-filter-layer",
 "Classic swept analog pad/lead: two detuned oscillators into a state-variable (SEM-style) filter driven by a triangle 'fin' filter envelope that rises then falls, modest resonance, mono mode with 8-voice stereo unison, light compression and tape echo, and an optional second oscillator pair through a different filter type swept the same way",
 "Recreate the classic 70s/80s filter-sweep sound from scratch. Use a low register and two oscillators slightly detuned for a phasing movement. Put them through a state-variable (SEM/Oberheim-type) lowpass and give the filter envelope a triangle 'fin' shape: a slow attack up and a slow decay back down with low sustain, so each note sweeps open and closes again; set envelope intensity, then add a little resonance (not too much). Switch to mono mode with full stereo unison (all 8 voices) and some detune for size. Finish with light compression and tape echo (no chorus needed). Optional: route oscillators 3–4 to a second, different filter type with its own fin envelope and detune, then blend both layers. More specific than our generic pad swell skills: up-and-down fin envelope on the filter plus mono unison stack.",
 ["Analog-style synth with a state-variable or multimode filter and filter envelope, or Web Audio: two detuned OscillatorNodes → BiquadFilter lowpass with frequency automated up then down (implementation note)","Unison/mono voice mode","Compressor and tape-style echo"],
 [("register","string","Low register for the sweep",True),("fin_attack","number","Filter envelope rise time (slow)",True),("fin_decay","number","Filter envelope fall time (slow), low sustain",True),("resonance","number","Moderate resonance",False)],
 [("sweep_patch","patch","Classic swept pad/lead"),("dual_filter_variant","patch","Two-filter layered version")],
 ["The video gives shapes, not numbers, except 8 unison voices. Set times and amounts by ear.","Vendor preset names from the video are not used. The SEM/Oberheim-type filter is a portable alias for a 12 dB state-variable lowpass.","Amp drive made little difference in the demo, so it is optional."],
 [("Init a patch. Choose a state-variable (SEM-style) lowpass, and if the synth offers vintage amp/envelope models, use them. Play in a low register with two oscillators slightly detuned.","osc + filter","Slow phasing between the two oscillators"),
  ("Shape the filter envelope as a triangle 'fin': slow rise to the peak, slow fall back down, low sustain. Set envelope intensity so the cutoff travels a wide range.","fin envelope","Each note sweeps up and then back down"),
  ("Add a little resonance, just enough to make the sweep sing without whistling.","resonance","Sweep becomes vocal and obvious"),
  ("Switch to mono mode with full stereo unison (8 voices) and add unison detune.","unison","Wide, thick single voice"),
  ("Add light compression and a tape echo. Skip chorus, because unison already gives width.","fx","Finished classic sweep with echo tail"),
  ("Optional: enable a second filter of a different type, send oscillators 3–4 to it, give it its own fin envelope and some detune, then blend both layers (pull back if it gets too dense).","dual filter","Two sweep characters layered")],
 ["Holding a note shows the cutoff rising and then falling without retriggering.","Turning resonance up makes the sweep peak clearly audible; turning it down returns a smooth sweep.","Mono + 8-voice unison sounds wider than the dual-oscillator patch alone."],
 ["filter-sweep","filter-envelope","state-variable-filter","unison","analog-pad","tape-echo","web-audio"],
 note="Short tutorial; preset name omitted.")
